## Slide 1

Interface Definitions

## Slide 2

Overview

Interface Definitions

Interface Definitions are defined as the pipe, or the way TrakSYS will transmit or receive a Payload.
There are a few types of Interface Definitions, including: 
Web Service Endpoint
Web Service Call
File Push
File Pull
Others
The Interface Definition Hub can be accessed from the Developer page nested under the Connect Hub. 
Interface Definitions can be organized in Groups for organization and maintainability.

## Slide 3

Types of Interfaces

Interface Definitions

Web Service
Endpoint / Call

File Service
Push / Pull

## Slide 4

Common Configuration

Interface Definitions

Linked Interface Definition
Typically, a reference to a parent interface definition

Massage Payload
The incoming payload definition

Response Payload
The outgoing payload definition

Complete Transaction in Success
Whether or not the interface transaction status is set to success after receiving payload and triggering post process actions.

Save Payload to Transaction
Whether or not payload message gets stored in transaction record

Key
Unique identifier of the Interface Definition. This is important as it will be used to reference the Interface Definition during data processing.

Name
Name of Interface Definition (non-programmatic)

Expiration Hours
How log transaction will persist in database
Module Trigger Key
DMS module to trigger after transaction
Process Definition Key
Workflow process to trigger after transaction

## Slide 5

Web Service Endpoint Configuration

Interface Definitions

Route
Running location of the Web Service
HTTP Method
Supported HTTP Methods: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE
Permission Set Key
Controls which user has permission to access the Web Service
Permission Item Key
Controls which user has permission to access the Web Service
Permission Action Key
Controls which user has permission to access the Web Service

## Slide 6

Web Service Endpoint URL

Interface Definitions

Generated URL
By clicking on the ‘Copy Endpoint URL’ button on the interface configuration screen, you’ll be able to see the webservice URL.

## Slide 7

Web Service Call Configuration

Interface Definitions

URL
Running location of the webservice
HTTP Method
Supported HTTP Methods: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE
Bearer Token
Token passed to an endpoint ensuring trusted connection

## Slide 8

File Pull Configuration

Interface Definitions

Root Folder Path
Location where file service is looking for named folders below
Login/Password
Security context for folder access
Source Folder Path
Location where file service is looking for new files
Success Folder Path
Location where file service will move successfully processed files
Failure Folder Path
Location where file service will move unsuccessfully processed files
Search Pattern
File name pattern file service will use when looking for new files
Max Files to Process
Number of files to process during a single pull (0 = no limit)

## Slide 9

File Push Configuration

Interface Definitions

Target Path
Location where file service will place output file
Login/Password
Security context for folder access
Target File Name
Name of file